  "transcript": "# Road Safety Strategy\n\n## Targets need stronger enforcement and funding\n> Witnesses agreed casualty targets are unlikely to be met without more enforcement capacity, better tools and extra funding.\n\nChair: Are the Government's casualty-reduction targets achievable without a big increase in enforcement?\nPaul Fermer: Short answer: no. Enforcement matters and needs to be the right type, but it's not the only element—we also need funding and community measures like Speedwatch.\nAngus Eaton: I agree enforcement is fundamental. It must be modernised; some penalties are outdated and the people and bodies who can enforce may need to change.\nJoy Allen: Yes. Also, different Government departments could pool effort and money: road safety saves lives and reduces costs across health, policing and social services, so joint funding makes sense.\n\n\n## Where to focus: speeds, rural roads and local limits\n> Lower urban and many rural speed limits, plus clearer national rules, were proposed to reduce deaths and injuries.\n\nChair: You said the strategy underplays safe speeds and roads—can you expand?\nPaul Fermer: Oxfordshire pushed 20 mph in many urban areas and saw benefits. We want 20 mph as the norm in communities and to reduce many A and B roads to 50 mph. It should be harder to justify higher limits.\nAngus Eaton: Speed limits must match the changing vehicle mix, especially new micromobility devices. Limits alone won’t help if those vehicles aren’t regulated or enforced.\nPaul Fermer: There’s confusion when different areas set different limits and satnavs disagree. A clearer national approach to default limits would reduce genuine confusion and non-compliance.\n\n\n## Data, partnerships and practical capacity gaps\n> Better data sharing, record-keeping and joined-up local partnerships are needed so enforcement can be targeted and preventative.\n\nAlex Mayer: What blocks effective joint working between police, DVLA and local authorities?\nJoy Allen: Data sharing. We also don’t record many non-serious collisions that show local danger. If minor crashes and near-misses were reported they’d give a fuller picture for prevention.\nAngus Eaton: Agree on data. Better, faster linking of insurance, MOT and tax data would help enforcement and predict problems. We also need legislative time for reforms.\nPaul Fermer: Partnerships work, but priorities and funding differ across police force areas. Councils lost road-safety capacity and are rebuilding it; police and councils need matched resource.\nJoe Morris: Does the system have enough officers to enforce current laws?\nJoy Allen: No. Roads policing is much reduced from a decade ago. But we can work smarter—Sweden keeps roads policing part of every officer's shift—and use partnerships and education better.\nAngus Eaton: It isn’t just officer numbers—it's tools. Initiatives like Tutelage, which bring data together to prioritise enforcement, need funding so police can be effective.\n\n\n## Enforcement tools: cameras, signage and uninsured driving\n> Average-speed cameras, better national rules on signage, and real-time insurance data would improve compliance and reduce harm.\n\nJoe Morris: How effective is the current system for enforcing speed limits?\nJoy Allen: A mix exists: fixed cameras, average-speed cameras and community Speedwatch. They work where used, but they need capital, deployment in the highest-risk spots and supporting police capacity.\nPaul Fermer: Average-speed camera programmes, like in Wales, change behaviour across a route better than single fixed cameras. But rollout is inconsistent and costly signs add maintenance overheads.\nChair: Does inconsistent local policy create sign clutter and extra cost?\nPaul Fermer: Yes. Non-default limits mean extra signs and upkeep. We tried to minimise costs by using existing poles, but patchy signage can also make enforcement harder.\nDr Arthur: On uninsured driving, how big is the problem?\nAngus Eaton: Uninsured driving costs the economy ~£1bn a year; the Bureau pays ~£260m annually in claims. Reserves for illegal or modified e-bike claims have jumped markedly and fuel continuing pressure.\nDr Arthur: Could real-time checks help the police spot uninsured vehicles?\nAngus Eaton: Yes. We need faster, less stale data so officers can check insurance at the roadside. The industry and agencies are working on it, but it will take time.\nJoy Allen: There are also grey areas: cars sold for parts or delivered unroadworthy can end up driven uninsured. Better responsibilities and data could close that gap.\n\n\n## Emerging threats: social media, micromobility and next steps\n> Social-media-fuelled dangerous driving, illegal modified e-bikes and unregulated micromobility are rising harms that need regulation, faster penalties and education.\n\nJacob Collier: How big an issue is dangerous driving pushed by social media?\nJoy Allen: It’s significant. People post high-speed or dangerous clips that others imitate; social media companies should take down content that facilitates crime quickly.\nAngus Eaton: We’re seeing a surge in claims from illegal or modified e-bikes; reserves have risen from £30m to £128m. Regulation of these vehicles would allow insurance and reduce harm.\nDr Arthur: Do education programmes work and could insurers require courses to reduce premiums?\nAngus Eaton: Education is effective and targeted programmes help. Insurers could consider behavioural rewards, but lowering premiums directly via education needs careful evidence.\nPaul Fermer: Beyond education, culture and ownership matter—drivers must recognise that getting in a vehicle means responsibility. Technology in vehicles could also limit speed if legislated.\nJoy Allen: My final ask: a fully funded delivery plan with clear accountability so PCCs, councils and police know who does what and have the resources to hit the targets.\nAngus Eaton: Regulate micromobility and be ready to review rules regularly. New vehicle types and offending patterns will keep emerging.\nPaul Fermer: Require local highway and police authorities to adopt a Vision Zero approach and publish clear local speed-limit principles so national intent meets local delivery.\nChair: Thank you. If there’s anything you want to add in writing, please do so; the Committee will continue this inquiry.\n",
